{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel went and did</b> — that they went out from before Moses and went to the flocks and performed the Pesach in the evening. And it is the way of Scripture to repeat and say <b>so they did</b>, to explain that they let nothing fall from all that they were commanded, as I explained concerning Noah (Genesis 6:22). And likewise, \"And Moses saw all the work, and behold, they had done it; as YHWH had commanded, so they did\" (Exodus 39:43). And our Sages gave a midrash on this (Mekhilta here): since Scripture did not need to mention the going, they said: to give reward for the going and reward for the doing. \"And they did\" — but had they not already done? Rather, since they accepted upon themselves to do it, Scripture reckoned it upon them as though they had done it. \"As He commanded Moses and Aaron, so they did\" — to declare the praise of Israel, that just as Moses and Aaron said to them, so they did. Another interpretation: what is there to learn from saying \"so they did\"? Rather, even Moses and Aaron did so. They expounded at the outset that the repetition is to praise Israel, that they did not forget and nothing fell away from them from all that was said to them, and this is the way of the expression in many places."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The verse citation in the Hebrew reads בראשית ו כב without chapter/verse punctuation marks; normalized to Genesis 6:22."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:06:25.306666073Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}